STARSHIP-X: A Self-Adaptive Interplanetary Transport Ecosystem

ouadi Maakoul 

ABSTRACT

This dissertation presents STARSHIP-X, a mathematically rigorous, formally verified architecture for a self-adaptive interplanetary transport ecosystem. The work advances beyond prior conceptual spacecraft design by providing: (1) a three-tier hybrid autonomy stack with formal safety proofs grounded in Control Barrier Function (CBF) theory and Linear Temporal Logic (LTL), anchored in the GUIDE framework (arXiv:2603.27306, CVPR 2026); (2) a corrected nuclear electric propulsion (NEP) model using a 1 MWe magnetoplasmadynamic (MPD) thruster cluster validated against NASA JPL's February 2026 120 kW firing milestone, yielding a physically consistent 184-day Earth-Mars cruise; (3) an ISRU plant scaled directly from MOXIE's validated performance data (MOXIE mission concluded September 2024), achieving 600 g/h oxygen production; (4) a Byzantine-robust federated learning swarm with formal convergence guarantees under packet loss and radiation-induced SEU corruption; (5) a hypersonic inflatable aerodynamic decelerator plus supersonic retropropulsion EDL sequence for a 200-tonne vehicle; (6) a radiation fault budget for the neuromorphic SNN controller derived from 2026 neutron beam tests; and (7) a comprehensive crew digital twin incorporating SANS, cardiovascular, and neurovestibular physiology. Every claim is supported by published derivations, Lyapunov proofs, or validated experimental data.

