## 2.3 Tier 2 — Reinforcement Learning with Vector Control Barrier Functions

### 2.3.1 Markov Decision Process Formulation

The reactive control problem is formulated as an MDP (S, A, P, R, γ) where:

S ⊆ R^n is the spacecraft state space, including relative position and velocity to obstacles, fuel remaining, body temperature at instrumented nodes, and structural load factor at critical joints.

A ⊆ R^m is the continuous action space of thrust vector commands and valve positions.

P: S × A × S → [0,1] is the transition distribution, approximated in a high-fidelity simulator.

R: S × A → R is the scalar reward, derived automatically from a co-safe LTL specification φ_mission.

γ ∈ (0,1) is the discount factor.

The RL policy is parameterised as a Gaussian: π_θ = N(μ_θ(s), Σ_θ(s)), trained with Proximal Policy Optimisation (PPO) in a MATLAB/Simulink high-fidelity simulator with full aerodynamic, thermal, and gravitational models.

### 2.3.2 LTL-Derived Reward

The mission specification is expressed in co-safe Linear Temporal Logic. For example, a docking-and-deorbit mission is written as:

φ = F(dock ∧ G(separation > 5 m)) ∧ G(¬collision)

where F is "eventually", G is "always", and ∧ is logical conjunction. The reward automaton is constructed from φ by translating it into a deterministic finite automaton (DFA) A_φ with states Q and transitions δ: Q × S → Q. The reward function is:

R(s, a) = R_automaton(q, q') + R_task(s, a)

where R_automaton(q, q') is +1 for a progress transition in the DFA (moving closer to the accepting state) and 0 otherwise. This approach, formalizing spacecraft tasks and safety requirements in Linear Temporal Logic, constructs reward functions automatically from co-safe LTL specifications for effective training, and provides probabilistic safety guarantees through the associated shield. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.05693)

### 2.3.3 Vector Control Barrier Function — Definition and Forward Invariance Proof

**Definition (vCBF):** Let h_i: S → R for i = 1...m be m continuously differentiable functions, each encoding one safety constraint:

h_1(s) = ||p_rel|| − d_min (minimum separation from obstacles)
h_2(s) = m_f − m_f_min (fuel margin above minimum reserve)
h_3(s) = σ_allow − σ_max(s) (structural stress below allowable)
h_4(s) = T_allow − T_max_body(s) (body temperature below limit)

The safe set is:

C = {s ∈ S : h_i(s) ≥ 0 for all i = 1...m}

**The Safety Filter (Quadratic Programme):**

u_safe = arg min_{u ∈ U} ||u − u_RL||_2^2

subject to: ∇h_i(s)·f(s,u) + α_i · h_i(s) ≥ 0 for all i = 1...m

where f(s,u) is the closed-loop vector field, α_i > 0 are class-K function parameters (here linear), and the left-hand side is the Lie derivative of h_i along the system trajectory.

**Theorem 1 (Forward Invariance of C under the vCBF Filter):** If the QP above is feasible at all s ∈ C and the system is governed by ẋ = f(x, u_safe), then C is forward invariant: if s(0) ∈ C then s(t) ∈ C for all t ≥ 0.

**Proof:** For each barrier h_i, by the QP constraint:

dh_i/dt = ∇h_i · f(s, u_safe) ≥ −α_i · h_i(s)

This is a first-order linear differential inequality. By the comparison lemma:

h_i(s(t)) ≥ h_i(s(0)) · e^{−α_i t}

Since h_i(s(0)) ≥ 0 for s(0) ∈ C, and e^{−α_i t} > 0 for all t, we conclude h_i(s(t)) ≥ 0 for all t ≥ 0 and all i. Therefore s(t) ∈ C for all t ≥ 0. □

This proof holds for each barrier independently. The joint safe set C = ∩_i {h_i ≥ 0} is forward invariant if all m QP constraints are simultaneously satisfiable. The combinatorial CBF framework, introducing an auxiliary variable for logical compositions of multiple CBFs, ensures that multiple constraints can be simultaneously enforced under a condition termed conjunctive compatibility, and the resulting CBF-based quadratic program is a continuous feedback law over the aggregated safe set. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.03536)

Conjunctive compatibility is verified offline for the STARSHIP-X operating envelope using the SMT solver dReal, confirming simultaneous feasibility at all sampled states in the simulation dataset.

### 2.3.4 RTA Integration into PPO Training Loop

During training, the safety filter is applied at every environment step, not only at deployment. This means the RL agent explores only within C, and its reward signal is never corrupted by unsafe trajectories. The empirical benefit, confirmed in the literature: using RTA during RL training reduces RTA activations from 6.85% to 0% while maintaining or improving task performance compared to post-hoc filtering. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.10530)

The training objective with the shield is:

L_PPO(θ) = E_{τ~π_θ}[min(r_t(θ)Â_t, clip(r_t(θ), 1−ε, 1+ε)Â_t)] − β·H[π_θ]

where the expectation is over shield-filtered trajectories only. The shield ensures the value function V_θ(s) accurately estimates expected return in safe regions, avoiding the optimistic bias that arises when unsafe trajectories are excluded only at deployment.

