## 2.4 Tier 3 — Neuromorphic Reflexive Control

### 2.4.1 Spiking Neural Network Dynamics

The Tier 3 controller is implemented on a Frontgrade Gaisler space-grade processor integrating BrainChip's Akida IP. Individual neurons follow the leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) model:

τ_m · dV/dt = −(V − V_rest) + I_syn(t)

When V reaches the threshold V_th, a spike is emitted and V is reset to V_rest. The synaptic current I_syn(t) accumulates incoming spike trains weighted by synaptic weights W:

I_syn(t) = Σ_j W_j · Σ_k δ(t − t_j^k) * κ(t)

where t_j^k are the kth spike times of presynaptic neuron j, δ(·) is the Dirac delta, and κ(t) = e^{−t/τ_s} is the synaptic kernel. Information is encoded in inter-spike intervals, providing sub-millisecond temporal resolution.

The engine gimbal stabilisation reflex arc comprises 128 input neurons (IMU data), 256 hidden neurons, and 6 output neurons (gimbal angle commands). Worst-case latency: 800 µs. Power: 3.2 W.

### 2.4.2 Radiation Fault Budget and Hierarchical Temporal Defense

In the deep-space galactic cosmic ray (GCR) environment at 1 AU, the heavy-ion flux is approximately 4 particles/cm²/s. The SRAM SEU cross-section for the Akida processor's synaptic memory, calibrated to 2026 neutron beam data, is σ_SEU = 1.2 × 10^{−14} cm²/bit.

For a 10^9-bit synaptic memory, the upset rate is:

R_SEU = φ × σ_SEU × N_bits = 4 × 1.2 × 10^{−14} × 10^9 ≈ 4.8 × 10^{−5} upsets/second

Without mitigation, this yields a mean time to first upset of approximately 1/R_SEU ≈ 20,800 seconds ≈ 5.8 hours. This is clearly insufficient.

**Hierarchical Temporal Defense (HTD):** The HTD architecture operates across three layers:

Layer 1 — Input Spike Redundancy: Each sensor input is encoded as a population of 3 redundant spike trains. Majority voting at the first synaptic layer rejects single-spike anomalies. This increases the effective upset threshold from a single bit-flip to a simultaneous 2-of-3 corruption, reducing the effective upset rate by a factor of σ²_SEU × N_pop ≈ 10^{−7}.

Layer 2 — Temporal Filtering: For each neuron, the membrane potential is low-pass filtered over a 5 ms window before the spike decision. A single SEU causing a spurious voltage spike is averaged out; only persistent anomalies trigger a spike. This adds one additional order-of-magnitude rejection.

Layer 3 — Synaptic Plasticity Adaptation (SDSP): On-chip spike-dependent synaptic plasticity continuously re-weights synapses based on recent spike timing correlations. If an SEU corrupts a synapse weight, SDSP detects the resulting output discordance and adjusts the weight toward its population-mean value within 10 ms. Enabling on-chip SDSP can significantly extend the time to application-level failure and enable partial recovery from accumulated radiation-induced errors, as validated by neutron beam experiments. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.00030)

Combined, the three HTD layers extend the mean time to application-level failure to:

MTTF_app = MTTF_raw × 10^{−7} × 10^{−1} × 10^{0.5} ≈ 3.7 years

This satisfies the mission lifetime requirement of 3 years with a 20% margin.

