# CHAPTER 4 — IN-SITU RESOURCE UTILISATION

## 4.1 Phase 1 — Corrected Solid-Oxide Electrolysis Plant

The MOXIE experiment, which concluded its successful mission on September 30, 2024, provides the validated scaling baseline. MOXIE ended its successful mission having satisfied all its success criteria and achieving twice its required oxygen production rate, suffering no failures over the course of its 16 operational runs. [USRA](https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/tenthmars2024/pdf/3057.pdf)

MOXIE's peak performance: oxygen production was sustained at 9.79 g/h for nearly 40 minutes, and MOXIE subsequently produced nearly 10.56 g/h at peak, with the note that a full-scale Mars mission version would produce oxygen nearly 200 times faster and work continuously for well over a year. [NASA Science](https://science.nasa.gov/blog/moxie-sets-consecutive-personal-bests-and-mars-records-for-oxygen-production/)

**Validated Scaling:** MOXIE mass = 15 kg, peak output = 10.56 g/h, power = 300 W. Specific power consumption: 300 W / 10.56 g/h = 28.4 W·h/g.

For the Phase 1 plant at 25 kW and 950 kg:
- Available power for electrolysis (80% system efficiency): 20 kW
- O₂ production: 20,000 W / 28.4 W·h·g^{−1} × 3600 s/h ... recalculating on an hourly basis: 20,000 W / (300 W / 10.56 g/h) = 20,000 / 28.4 W per g/h = **704 g/h**

This is rounded to 600 g/h as a conservative operational figure accounting for stack degradation at the 950 kg scale. The v2.0 figure of 20 g/h was non-physical by a factor of 30.

**Dust-Storm Resilience:** A 5 kWe Kilopower-class reactor (KRUSTY-derived, 100 kg) provides primary power to the plant during global dust storms. A 300 kWh battery provides 3-day transition power during reactor startup.

## 4.2 Break-Even Analysis with Launch Cost Sensitivity

The mass multiplier is η = M_saved / M_ISRU. The economic value of ISRU depends critically on launch cost. We compute η for three scenarios:

Let m_O2 = total oxygen produced over 5-year plant life = 600 g/h × 8760 h/yr × 5 yr × 0.85 availability = 22,338 kg O₂.

M_ISRU = 950 kg plant + 100 kg reactor = 1,050 kg.

| Launch cost ($/kg to Mars surface) | Value of O₂ saved ($) | η |
|---|---|---|
| $1,000 | $22.3M | 21.3 |
| $100 | $2.23M | 2.13 |
| $10 | $0.22M | 0.21 |

At Starship-class launch costs (~$100/kg), Phase 1 ISRU breaks even with η ≈ 2. At $10/kg, ISRU for oxygen alone is economically marginal. However, the strategic rationale — logistic independence from Earth, insurance against supply chain failure — justifies the investment even below the economic break-even threshold, particularly as Phase 2 adds water and metals production.

## 4.3 Phase 2 Mining Throughput Validation

CRISM/OMEGA orbital spectroscopy data for equatorial Mars constrains near-surface water-ice content to 2–5 wt% at depths < 1 m. Using the conservative value of 3 wt%:

Mass of regolith to excavate per sol to produce 500 kg water:
M_regolith = 500 kg / 0.03 = 16,667 kg/sol

The rover fleet comprises 3 IPEx-type rovers (800 kg each) with counter-rotating bucket-drum excavators. Each rover achieves 2 t/h excavation rate in low-gravity (0.38 g) Martian regolith using the traction force model:

F_t = c·A + W·tan(φ)

where c = 1.5 kPa (Martian regolith cohesion from InSight seismology data), A = 0.08 m² (bucket contact area), W = 800 kg × 0.38 × 9.81 = 2,979 N (normal load in Martian gravity), φ = 35° (internal friction angle from Viking and Curiosity soil mechanics). This gives F_t = 120 + 2,979 × 0.70 = 2,205 N per rover, sufficient for 2 t/h throughput.

Total fleet capacity over 16-hour operating day:
M_total = 3 rovers × 2 t/h × 16 h = **96 t/sol**

This is 5.8× the required 16.7 t/sol, providing ample margin for downtime, maintenance, and dust accumulation. The fleet was undersized in v2.0 (3 × 500 kg rovers); the correction to 3 × 800 kg provides 60% additional throughput per rover through larger bucket drums.

