# CHAPTER 5 — SWARM COORDINATION, FEDERATED LEARNING, AND CYBERSECURITY

## 5.1 Federated Learning Formulation

Each of N spacecraft holds a local model w_i (e.g., a neural network for thruster degradation prediction) trained on its private, non-shared telemetry dataset D_i. The global model w* minimises the federated objective:

F(w) = Σ_{i=1}^{N} (n_i/n) · F_i(w)

where F_i(w) = (1/n_i) Σ_{x∈D_i} ℓ(w; x) is the local loss, n_i = |D_i|, and n = Σ_i n_i.

**FedProx Update:** To handle non-IID data (each spacecraft has unique operating history), each local update includes a proximal regularisation term:

w_i^{t+1} = arg min_w {F_i(w) + (μ/2)||w − w^t||²}

where μ > 0 is the proximal coefficient. This penalises local models from deviating too far from the global model, maintaining global coherence even when local data distributions differ significantly. FedProx provides convergence guarantees for heterogeneous networks and demonstrates significantly more stable and accurate convergence behaviour relative to FedAvg in highly heterogeneous settings, improving absolute test accuracy by 22% on average. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.06127)

The global aggregation step is:

w^{t+1} = Σ_{i=1}^{N} (n_i/n) · w_i^{t+1}

## 5.2 Byzantine-Robust Aggregation — Theorem and Proof

In the deep-space swarm, single-event upsets can corrupt local model weights before transmission, producing Byzantine updates — updates that are arbitrary and potentially far outside the range of valid gradients. If even one vehicle transmits a corrupted model update, standard FedAvg can diverge.

**Coordinate-Wise Trimmed Mean (CWTM) Aggregation:**

For each parameter dimension j:

w_j^{t+1} = TrimMean(w_{1,j}^{t+1}, ..., w_{N,j}^{t+1}; β)

where TrimMean removes the top β and bottom β fraction of values before averaging. For our swarm of N = 5 vehicles, β = 0.2 (remove the top and bottom vehicle update), leaving 3 vehicles' updates per dimension.

**Theorem 2 (Convergence of CWTM under Byzantine Attacks):** Assume F is L-smooth and each local F_i is μ-strongly convex. Let f_b ≤ β be the fraction of Byzantine vehicles. Then with learning rate η_t = η_0/√t, the CWTM aggregated sequence satisfies:

||w^T − w*||² ≤ (||w^0 − w*||²/√T) + O(σ² + δ²·β²)

where σ² is the gradient variance of benign clients and δ is the maximum Byzantine perturbation magnitude. The error floor O(δ²β²) vanishes as β → 0.

**Proof sketch:** At each round t, the CWTM estimator of the true gradient ∇F(w^t) satisfies:

||CWTM_gradient − ∇F(w^t)||² ≤ 4β² · ||∇F(w^t)||² + 2σ²/N_benign

This follows from the Yin et al. (2018) trimmed-mean error bound. Substituting into the gradient descent recursion and summing over T rounds yields the stated bound by standard L-smooth analysis. Rigorous proof shows that zero optimality gap is achieved by geometric-median robust aggregation with linear convergence, as long as the fraction of Byzantine attackers is below one-half. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.10427)

For CWTM, our bound requires f_b < β = 0.2. In a 5-vehicle swarm, this means at most 1 vehicle can be corrupted — a reasonable assumption for a fleet where radiation events are rare and SEU detection is built into the hardware.

**Physics-Based Update Sanity Check:** Before CWTM aggregation, each received update w_i is validated against a physics model. For thruster degradation, the model predicts that specific impulse degrades monotonically: dI_sp/dt ≤ 0. Any update predicting positive I_sp growth is rejected. This rejects physically impossible Byzantine corruption without requiring knowledge of the attack strategy.

## 5.3 Formation Control — Passivity-Based Stability Proof

For a swarm of N vehicles with positions p_i ∈ R³ and masses m_i, the desired formation is specified by offset vectors d_{ij} for each edge (i,j) in the communication graph G.

**Control Law:**

u_i = −Σ_{j∈N_i} K_p(p_i − p_j − d_{ij}) − K_d · ṗ_i

where K_p > 0 and K_d > 0 are positive definite gain matrices.

**Theorem 3 (Asymptotic Formation Stability):** Under the above control law, the formation error e_{ij} = p_i − p_j − d_{ij} converges to zero asymptotically, i.e., lim_{t→∞} e_{ij}(t) = 0 for all (i,j).

**Proof:** Define the Lyapunov storage function:

V = (1/2) Σ_i m_i ||ṗ_i||² + (1/2) Σ_{(i,j)∈E} K_p ||e_{ij}||²

The first term is the kinetic energy of formation errors; the second is a quadratic potential. Computing the time derivative:

V̇ = Σ_i m_i ṗ_i^T p̈_i + Σ_{(i,j)∈E} K_p e_{ij}^T ė_{ij}

Substituting m_i p̈_i = u_i = −Σ_j K_p e_{ij} − K_d ṗ_i:

V̇ = −Σ_i K_d ||ṗ_i||² + Σ_i ṗ_i^T (−Σ_j K_p e_{ij}) + Σ_{(i,j)} K_p e_{ij}^T (ṗ_i − ṗ_j)

The last two terms cancel through graph-algebraic manipulation (the sum telescopes over edges in G):

V̇ = −Σ_i K_d ||ṗ_i||² ≤ 0

Since V is positive definite and V̇ ≤ 0, V is a Lyapunov function. Since V̇ = 0 only when all ṗ_i = 0, LaSalle's invariance principle guarantees convergence to the largest invariant set where V̇ = 0, which is exactly the desired formation {e_{ij} = 0, ṗ_i = 0}. □

**Passivity and Delay Robustness:** The system is passive from the exogenous disturbance w to the error output z with storage function V. Passivity implies that communication delays τ_{ij} < π/(2ω_n) (where ω_n = √(K_p/m) is the natural frequency of the formation) preserve stability. For K_p/m = 0.01 (low-gain setting for deep-space formation), ω_n ≈ 0.1 rad/s, tolerating delays up to 15.7 seconds. This comfortably covers the Mars local swarm's UHF round-trip time of < 1 second.

## 5.4 DCOP Convergence under DTN Contact Schedule

The resource allocation problem (fuel, oxygen, computational tasks) is formulated as a Distributed Constraint Optimisation Problem (DCOP). Each agent i controls variable x_i (fuel to transfer). The factor graph has factors f_j encoding constraints between pairs of agents.

**Max-Sum Algorithm:**

message_{i→j}(x_j) = max_{x_i} [f_i(x_i) + Σ_{k∈N_i\j} message_{k→i}(x_i) + c_{ij}(x_i, x_j)]

For the Mars swarm, contact windows are 25 minutes every 100 minutes (orbital period). The DTN contact schedule defines a time-expanded graph G_T. We prove:

**Theorem 4 (DCOP Convergence under DTN):** With the tree-reparameterisation method applied to G_T, the Max-Sum algorithm converges to a feasible allocation within ⌈log(N)⌉ contact windows with probability at least 1 − N · p_loss^{N_window}, where p_loss is the per-message loss probability.

For N = 5 vehicles, p_loss = 0.05, N_window = 5 orbit periods: probability of convergence ≥ 1 − 5 × (0.05)^5 ≈ 0.9997. This exceeds the 0.98 target.

## 5.5 Cybersecurity Architecture

All inter-vehicle and vehicle-ground communications are secured at the Bundle Protocol Security (BPSec) layer:

Encryption: AES-256-GCM, 256-bit keys, authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD). Provides both confidentiality and integrity.

Authentication: ECDSA with NIST P-384 curve. Each spacecraft has a unique private key generated and certified at launch. Signatures on all DTN bundles prevent replay attacks.

Key Establishment: Quantum-resistant key exchange performed pre-launch using CRYSTALS-Kyber (NIST PQC standard). This future-proofs the communication security against quantum adversaries throughout the mission's 10-year lifespan.

Certificate Revocation: A compact Bloom filter CRL is synchronised across the swarm at each contact window, allowing detection of compromised vehicles within one orbit period.

Federated Learning Poisoning Defence: As described in Section 5.2, CWTM + physics validation constitutes the model-layer security. The cryptographic layer prevents an external attacker from injecting false updates; the CWTM layer tolerates radiation-corrupted internal updates.

