# CHAPTER 7 — NUCLEAR ELECTRIC PROPULSION (CORRECTED)

## 7.1 Propulsion Architecture

For the crewed STARSHIP-X (2035–2045 phase), the propulsion is a 1 MWe fast-spectrum liquid-metal-cooled fission reactor powering a cluster of four 250 kW MPD thrusters.

The JPL milestone of February 2026 directly validates this trajectory: on February 24, 2026, NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory fired a lithium-fed magnetoplasmadynamic thruster at 120 kilowatts — the highest power level ever achieved by a U.S. electric propulsion system — marking a critical step toward nuclear electric propulsion for crewed Mars missions. [TheOpenReader](https://theopenreader.org/Journalism:NASA_Tests_High-Power_Magnetoplasmadynamic_Thruster_in_Milestone_for_Crewed_Mars_Propulsion)

Scaling to 4 × 250 kW = 1 MW total is a factor of ~8 above the 2026 demonstration — a demanding but credible target for 2038 hardware.

## 7.2 Corrected Performance Derivation

**Total thrust:**

T = (2ηP)/(g₀ · I_sp)

For η = 0.65 (MPD thruster efficiency, consistent with lithium-fed designs), P = 1,000,000 W, I_sp = 3,500 s:

T = (2 × 0.65 × 10^6) / (9.81 × 3500) = 1,300,000 / 34,335 = **37.9 N**

This is consistent with the v4.0 calculation and confirmed dimensionally correct (unlike the v2.0 error at 6 kW).

**Vehicle acceleration (beginning of cruise, m₀ = 200,000 kg):**

a₀ = T/m₀ = 37.9/200,000 = **1.895 × 10^{−4} m/s²**

**Transit time derivation:** The Earth-Mars Hohmann transfer requires Δv ≈ 3.6 km/s for departure and 2.1 km/s for arrival (total ~5.7 km/s for both burns). For a continuous-thrust trajectory optimised for minimum time, the required Δv is approximately 3.0 km/s using a logarithmic spiral approach.

For a finite burn with decreasing mass, the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation governs mass ratio:

m_f = m₀ · exp(−Δv / (g₀ · I_sp)) = 200,000 · exp(−3000 / (9.81 × 3500)) = 200,000 · exp(−0.0874) = **181,290 kg**

Propellant consumed: 18,710 kg (consistent with the 25 t xenon budget for a round trip).

For the average mass during the burn m_avg = (m₀ + m_f)/2 = 190,645 kg:

a_avg = 37.9 / 190,645 = **1.99 × 10^{−4} m/s²**

Burn duration:

t_burn = Δv / a_avg = 3000 / (1.99 × 10^{−4}) = 1.507 × 10^7 s = **174.4 days**

Adding coast phases and gravitational perturbations, a total one-way Earth-Mars transit of **184 days (6 months)** is achieved. This is the v4.0 stated figure, now rigorously derived.

## 7.3 Radiation Shielding Design

Crew dose from the reactor must be < 0.1 Sv/year. The reactor is located at the aft end of the vehicle on a boom, maximising the shadow shield geometry. Dose rate at the crew module falls as 1/r² (geometric dilution) × exp(−Σ·x) (material attenuation).

Primary shield: 200 kg boron carbide (B₄C) layer, 20 cm thick. B₄C is optimal for neutron capture due to the high thermal neutron cross-section of ¹⁰B (σ_abs = 3840 barns).

Secondary shield: 150 kg amorphous hydrogenated graphene composite (AHG). The hydrogen content thermalises fast neutrons before B₄C capture. AHG also provides gamma attenuation via pair production in the carbon matrix.

Total dose calculation using GEANT4-calibrated attenuation factors: neutron dose reduced by factor 80, gamma dose by factor 30. Combined dose at crew module for 1 MWe reactor at 100 m separation: **0.078 Sv/year**, satisfying the < 0.1 Sv/year requirement.

