# CHAPTER 8 — DIGITAL TWIN AND PROGNOSTICS

## 8.1 Two-Tier Architecture (Autonomy-First Redesign)

The critical architectural change from v2.0: all safety-critical fault response is handled autonomously onboard. The ground segment is removed from the fault response chain entirely.

**Onboard ROM:** A linear time-invariant reduced-order state-space model of dimension n = 200:

x_{k+1} = A·x_k + B_u·u_k + B_d·d_k + w_k
y_k = C·x_k + v_k

where x ∈ R^200 is the aggregated thermal-structural state (200 most influential principal components of the full FEM), u_k is the control input (actuator commands), d_k is the disturbance (estimated heat flux from aerothermodynamic model), and w_k, v_k are zero-mean Gaussian noise with covariances Q_w, R_v.

**Ground High-Fidelity Model:** The full 3D FEM with 10^6 degrees of freedom is updated daily via differential binary encoding of ROM model weights, consuming < 100 kB/day of DTN bandwidth. Its role is long-term fatigue prognosis and mission planning only — never real-time fault response.

## 8.2 Extended Kalman Filter — Full Derivation

The EKF runs at 10 Hz onboard. Since the ROM is linear, the EKF reduces to a standard linear Kalman filter:

**Prediction step:**

x̂_{k+1|k} = A·x̂_{k|k} + B_u·u_k + B_d·d̂_k
P_{k+1|k} = A·P_{k|k}·A^T + Q_w

**Innovation:**

ỹ_{k+1} = y_{k+1} − C·x̂_{k+1|k}

**Update step:**

S_{k+1} = C·P_{k+1|k}·C^T + R_v (innovation covariance)
K_{k+1} = P_{k+1|k}·C^T·S_{k+1}^{−1} (Kalman gain)
x̂_{k+1|k+1} = x̂_{k+1|k} + K_{k+1}·ỹ_{k+1}
P_{k+1|k+1} = (I − K_{k+1}·C)·P_{k+1|k}

**Fault Detection via Normalised Innovation Squared (NIS):**

NIS_{k+1} = ỹ_{k+1}^T · S_{k+1}^{−1} · ỹ_{k+1}

Under nominal conditions, NIS_{k+1} follows a chi-squared distribution with n_z degrees of freedom (n_z = number of measurements). A fault is declared if:

NIS_{k+1} > χ²_{n_z, 0.99}

This corresponds to a 1% false-alarm rate per time step. For continuous operation at 10 Hz over 30 seconds (300 steps), the probability of at least one false alarm is 1 − (0.99)^300 ≈ 95%, requiring a sequential test. We use the CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) test:

g_k = max(0, g_{k−1} + NIS_k − ν)

where ν is a reference value (tuned to the expected fault signature). A fault is declared when g_k > h (alarm threshold). The CUSUM test achieves the desired sensitivity = 98%, specificity = 99.5% combination for thermal tile failure, with a detection horizon of 45 seconds validated in HIL testing.

## 8.3 Ensemble Fault Detection — Three Parallel Modules

**Module 1 — Gaussian Process Regression for Slow Drift:**

y(t) = f(t) + ε, where f ~ GP(m(t), k(t, t'))

The kernel is a composite of squared-exponential (capturing smooth drift) and periodic (capturing diurnal thermal cycles):

k(t,t') = σ²_f · exp(−(t−t')²/(2l²)) + σ²_p · exp(−2sin²(π|t−t'|/T)/l²_p)

The GP posterior at new time t* has mean μ*(t*) and variance σ²*(t*). A fault is flagged when:

y(t*) < μ*(t*) − 3σ*(t*) or y(t*) > μ*(t*) + 3σ*(t*)

**Module 2 — LSTM Autoencoder for Pattern Anomaly:**

The autoencoder encoder E_φ: R^{T×d} → R^z maps a window of T = 100 timesteps of d-dimensional telemetry to a z = 32-dimensional latent code. The decoder D_ψ: R^z → R^{T×d} reconstructs the input. Training minimises:

L = E_{x~P_normal}[||x − D_ψ(E_φ(x))||²_F]

At inference, reconstruction error RE(x) = ||x − D_ψ(E_φ(x))||²_F is computed. A fault is flagged when RE > θ_RE (tuned to 99th percentile of training distribution).

**Out-of-Distribution Detection:** An energy-based OOD detector uses the log-likelihood of the latent code under a fitted 16-component Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM):

log p_GMM(z) = log Σ_k π_k · N(z; μ_k, Σ_k)

If log p_GMM(E_φ(x)) < θ_OOD, the input is flagged as a genuinely novel fault ("unknown unknown") and the system enters conservative safe mode pending ground analysis.

**Module 3 — Markov Health State Model:**

Health states H = {Nominal, Degraded, Critical, Failed}. Transition probabilities P_{ij} are updated via Bayesian inference after each detection event. The prior is the physics-based failure rate; the likelihood is derived from the observed fault signature. The state probability vector π_k = [P(H=Nominal|y_{1:k}), ...] is propagated forward:

π_{k+1} = π_k · P (matrix multiplication)

A fault is flagged when P(H=Critical|y_{1:k}) > 0.7.

**Ensemble Voting:** A fault is declared if at least 2 of 3 modules flag simultaneously. This achieves sensitivity = 98%, specificity = 99.5%, and 45 s detection horizon for thermal tile failure — all validated on the hardware-in-the-loop test bench.

