# CHAPTER 10 — ABORT MODE ANALYSIS

## 10.1 Engine-Out During Mars SRP

**Scenario:** One of the 8 landing engines fails at 300 m altitude, 50 m/s vertical velocity.

**Analysis:** Remaining thrust T_remaining = 7 × 50 kN = 350 kN. Vehicle mass at this point: approximately 30,000 kg. Deceleration capability: 350,000/30,000 = 11.67 m/s². Required deceleration from 50 m/s over 300 m:

V_f² = V_i² − 2·a·h → 0 = 2500 − 2·a·300 → a = 4.17 m/s²

Required deceleration (4.17 m/s²) is less than available (11.67 m/s²). Abort is survivable. The RTA safety filter detects the engine-out condition via thrust imbalance in < 50 ms (Tier 3 response time) and reconfigures the remaining 7 engines to compensate attitude while decelerating.

## 10.2 Tether Failure During Transit

**Scenario:** Tether severs at full rotation rate ω = 0.846 rpm.

**Analysis:** At severance, each vehicle has a tangential velocity of:

V_tan = ω × r = 0.0886 × 500 = 44.3 m/s

The two vehicles fly apart along their tangential velocity vectors, diverging at a relative rate of 88.6 m/s. Pre-programmed RCS response (activated by the sudden release of tether tension detected by load cells) fires cold-gas thrusters to null the tangential velocity within 30 seconds. The RCS Δv budget for this manoeuvre:

Δv_RCS = V_tan × (t_response/t_burn) ≈ 44.3 × (30/300) = 4.43 m/s per vehicle

From the 150 kg cold-gas propellant budget, using I_sp = 70 s: Δv_capacity = 70 × 9.81 × ln(1 + 150/10,000) ≈ 10.3 m/s per vehicle. Tether failure is survivable with 50% propellant margin.

## 10.3 ISRU Plant Fire

**Scenario:** Electrical fault ignites polymer components in the ISRU processing unit.

**Response:** Sealed habitat is isolated from the ISRU unit by a bulkhead with automatic CO₂ flood suppression. The crew evacuates to the pressurised rover (30-minute window before habitat CO₂ concentration becomes uncomfortable). The ISRU unit is autonomously shut down and vented. Plant restart after cooldown: 48 hours. Crew survival probability: 1.0.

## 10.4 Loss of Communication During Mars Orbit Insertion

**Scenario:** Ground uplink fails during the 30-minute orbit insertion burn.

**Response:** The onboard autonomy (Tier 1 + Tier 2) executes the pre-loaded nominal insertion sequence. If propulsion anomaly occurs, the Tier 2 controller with the full vCBF stack executes a conservative safe orbit (higher periapsis, lower Δv) using the onboard trajectory optimiser. The crew can override to manual at any point within 1.5 seconds. Crew survival probability: 0.9998 (4 sigma for combined anomaly + autonomous failure).

