# APPENDIX C — LINEAR TEMPORAL LOGIC AND REWARD SYNTHESIS

## C.1 LTL Syntax and Semantics

**Definition C.1 (LTL Syntax).** Given a set of atomic propositions AP, LTL formulae over AP are generated by:

φ ::= p | ¬φ | φ₁ ∧ φ₂ | X φ | φ₁ U φ₂

where p ∈ AP, X is "next" (next time step), U is "until". Derived operators:

F φ = true U φ (eventually φ)
G φ = ¬F(¬φ) (always φ)
φ₁ R φ₂ = ¬(¬φ₁ U ¬φ₂) (φ₂ holds until and unless φ₁ releases it)

**Definition C.2 (LTL Semantics — Trace-Based).** For an infinite word w = w₀w₁w₂... ∈ (2^{AP})^ω and position i ≥ 0:

w, i ⊨ p iff p ∈ w_i
w, i ⊨ ¬φ iff w, i ⊭ φ
w, i ⊨ φ₁ ∧ φ₂ iff w, i ⊨ φ₁ and w, i ⊨ φ₂
w, i ⊨ Xφ iff w, i+1 ⊨ φ
w, i ⊨ φ₁ U φ₂ iff ∃j ≥ i: w, j ⊨ φ₂ and ∀k: i ≤ k < j: w, k ⊨ φ₁

A trace w satisfies φ (written w ⊨ φ) iff w, 0 ⊨ φ.

**Definition C.3 (Co-Safe LTL).** A formula φ is co-safe if there exists a finite good prefix — a finite word u such that for all infinite extensions uω, we have uω ⊨ φ. Examples: F p, F(p ∧ Fq), p U q. These formulae describe properties that can be witnessed in finite time. All STARSHIP-X mission objective formulae are co-safe.

**Definition C.4 (Safe LTL).** A formula φ is safe if every violating trace has a finite bad prefix — a finite word u such that for all infinite extensions uω, we have uω ⊭ φ. Examples: G p, G(p → Gq). All STARSHIP-X safety constraints are safe.

---

## C.2 DFA Construction from Co-Safe LTL

**Algorithm C.1 (LTL-to-DFA via Spot).** Given co-safe formula φ:

Step 1. Translate φ to a non-deterministic Büchi automaton (NBA) using the standard LTL-to-NBA algorithm (Vardi-Wolper construction). NBA has states Q_B, alphabet 2^{AP}, transitions Δ_B, initial state q₀_B, and accepting set F_B.

Step 2. Determinise the NBA to a deterministic Rabin automaton (DRA) using Safra's construction: DRA has states Q_D ⊆ 2^{Q_B}, alphabet 2^{AP}, and acceptance pairs {(E_i, F_i)}.

Step 3. Since φ is co-safe, the DRA simplifies to a DFA with a single accepting (trap) state q_acc and a single rejecting (trap) state q_rej. All accepting states in DRA collapse to q_acc.

The resulting DFA A_φ = (Q, q₀, Σ, δ, q_acc) tracks the progress of the mission trace toward satisfying φ.

**Reward Automaton Construction:**

R_automaton(q, σ, q') = {
  +1 if δ(q, σ) = q' and this transition strictly progresses toward q_acc
  0 otherwise
  −∞ if q = q_rej (terminal penalty, not used in practice; instead episode terminates)
}

The RL agent receives R_automaton at each step, driving it to satisfy φ in minimal time.

---

## C.3 Shield Construction from Safe LTL

For safety formula ψ = G(p_safe) (always safe), the shield is constructed as:

**Algorithm C.2 (Shield from Safe LTL).**

Step 1. Construct the safety automaton A_ψ = (Q_s, q₀_s, Σ, δ_s, F_s) where F_s = Q_s \ {q_unsafe} (all states except the error state are accepting).

Step 2. For each state q ∈ Q_s and each state **x** of the MDP, define the shield preimage:

Shield_safe(q, **x**) = {**a** ∈ A: δ_s(q, L(**x**)) ≠ q_unsafe}

where L: S → 2^{AP} is the labelling function mapping physical states to atomic propositions.

Step 3. The shield-filtered action is:

**u**_shield = **u**_RL if **u**_RL ∈ Shield_safe(q, **x**)
**u**_shield = any **u** ∈ Shield_safe(q, **x**) otherwise (closest in L2 norm)

**Theorem C.1 (Shield Safety Guarantee).** Under shield-filtered control, for any RL policy π, the probability of violating ψ is exactly 0 (deterministic shield) or at most p_fail (probabilistic shield with known transition model).

**Proof.** If **u**_shield always satisfies **u**_shield ∈ Shield_safe(q, **x**), then by construction δ_s(q, L(**x**)) ≠ q_unsafe for all transitions, so the safety automaton never enters q_unsafe. Since ψ = G(p_safe) is violated iff q_unsafe is reached, the formula is never violated. □

