# APPENDIX D — KALMAN FILTERING AND STATE ESTIMATION

## D.1 Kalman Filter Derivation from First Principles

**Problem Statement.** Consider the discrete-time linear system:

**x**_{k+1} = **A****x**_k + **B****u**_k + **w**_k, **w**_k ~ N(**0**, **Q**)
**y**_k = **C****x**_k + **v**_k, **v**_k ~ N(**0**, **R**)

with **w** and **v** independent. The Kalman filter computes the minimum mean-square-error (MMSE) estimate **x̂**_{k|k} = E[**x**_k | **y**_{1:k}].

**Derivation.**

We seek the linear estimator **x̂**_{k|k} = **K**_k **y**_k + some function of prior, that minimises E[||**x**_k − **x̂**_{k|k}||²].

**Step 1 — Prior Distribution.** Given **x̂**_{k|k−1} (prior estimate before observing **y**_k) and P_{k|k−1} (prior error covariance):

p(**x**_k | **y**_{1:k−1}) = N(**x̂**_{k|k−1}, **P**_{k|k−1})

**Step 2 — Likelihood.** The measurement likelihood is:

p(**y**_k | **x**_k) = N(**C****x**_k, **R**)

**Step 3 — Posterior (Bayes' theorem).** Since both distributions are Gaussian, the posterior is Gaussian:

p(**x**_k | **y**_{1:k}) ∝ p(**y**_k | **x**_k) · p(**x**_k | **y**_{1:k−1})

Computing the product of Gaussian pdfs (completing the square in **x**_k):

p(**x**_k | **y**_{1:k}) = N(**x̂**_{k|k}, **P**_{k|k})

where the posterior mean and covariance are:

**S**_k = **C****P**_{k|k−1}**C**^T + **R** (innovation covariance)
**K**_k = **P**_{k|k−1}**C**^T **S**_k^{−1} (Kalman gain)
**x̂**_{k|k} = **x̂**_{k|k−1} + **K**_k(**y**_k − **C****x̂**_{k|k−1})
**P**_{k|k} = (**I** − **K**_k **C**)**P**_{k|k−1}

**Optimality Proof:** The gain **K**_k that minimises tr(**P**_{k|k}) is found by:

d/d**K** tr(**P**_{k|k}) = d/d**K** tr[(**I** − **KC**)**P**_{k|k−1}(**I** − **KC**)^T + **KRK**^T] = 0

Expanding and differentiating using matrix calculus identities (d tr(**AXB**)/d**X** = **B****A**^T):

−2**P**_{k|k−1}**C**^T + 2**K**(**CP**_{k|k−1}**C**^T + **R**) = **0**

Solving: **K**_k = **P**_{k|k−1}**C**^T(**CP**_{k|k−1}**C**^T + **R**)^{−1} = **P**_{k|k−1}**C**^T **S**_k^{−1} □

**Time Prediction Step:**

**x̂**_{k+1|k} = **A****x̂**_{k|k} + **B****u**_k
**P**_{k+1|k} = **A****P**_{k|k}**A**^T + **Q**

**Convergence of Error Covariance.** Under observability and controllability conditions, **P**_{k|k} converges to a steady-state value **P**_∞ satisfying the discrete algebraic Riccati equation (DARE):

**P**_∞ = **A**[**P**_∞ − **P**_∞**C**^T(**CP**_∞**C**^T + **R**)^{−1}**CP**_∞]**A**^T + **Q**

The steady-state Kalman gain **K**_∞ = **P**_∞**C**^T(**CP**_∞**C**^T + **R**)^{−1} can be pre-computed offline, saving onboard computation.

---

## D.2 Normalised Innovation Squared Test

**Definition D.1 (NIS).** The Normalised Innovation Squared is:

NIS_k = **ỹ**_k^T **S**_k^{−1} **ỹ**_k where **ỹ**_k = **y**_k − **C****x̂**_{k|k−1}

**Theorem D.1 (NIS Distribution Under Null Hypothesis).** If the system model is correct and no fault is present, NIS_k ~ χ²(n_y), a chi-squared distribution with n_y degrees of freedom (n_y = dim(**y**_k)).

**Proof.** Under the null hypothesis, **ỹ**_k ~ N(**0**, **S**_k). Therefore **S**_k^{−1/2} **ỹ**_k ~ N(**0**, **I**). The NIS is the sum of squares of n_y independent standard normal variables:

NIS_k = (**S**_k^{−1/2} **ỹ**_k)^T (**S**_k^{−1/2} **ỹ**_k) = Σ_{i=1}^{n_y} z_i² where z_i ~ N(0,1) i.i.d.

By definition, Σ z_i² ~ χ²(n_y). □

**Fault Detection Rule:** Declare fault if NIS_k > χ²_{n_y, 1−α} (the 1−α quantile of the chi-squared distribution). For n_y = 10 measurements (thermocouple channels) and α = 0.01 (1% false alarm rate per step): χ²_{10, 0.99} = 23.2.

**CUSUM Sequential Test (for sustained faults):**

g_k = max(0, g_{k−1} + NIS_k − ν)

where ν = χ²_{n_y, 0.5} = n_y (median under null hypothesis). Declare fault when g_k > h. The CUSUM test has optimal (minimum expected detection delay) properties for a given false-alarm rate — proven by the Lorden/Moustakides theorems. For STARSHIP-X, ν = 10 and h = 50 gives mean detection delay of 4.5 steps (0.45 s at 10 Hz) for a 3σ step-change fault, with average run length 1000 steps (100 s) between false alarms.

---

## D.3 Reduced-Order Model Construction

The full FEM has dimension n_full ~ 10^6 degrees of freedom. The ROM is constructed by Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD):

**Step 1.** Run the full FEM for N_snap = 500 snapshot scenarios (nominal and off-nominal thermal loads). Collect the snapshot matrix:

**X**_snap = [**x**_1, **x**_2, ..., **x**_{N_snap}] ∈ R^{n_full × N_snap}

**Step 2.** Compute the SVD: **X**_snap = **U****Σ****V**^T. Select the first r = 200 columns of **U** (the POD basis **Φ** ∈ R^{n_full × r}).

**Energy criterion:** The retained energy fraction is:

η_r = Σ_{i=1}^r σ_i² / Σ_{i=1}^{n_full} σ_i² ≥ 0.999

This ensures 99.9% of the thermal energy variation is captured in the 200-dimensional ROM.

**Step 3.** Project the FEM onto the ROM basis:

**A**_ROM = **Φ**^T **A**_FEM **Φ** ∈ R^{200×200}
**B**_ROM = **Φ**^T **B**_FEM ∈ R^{200×n_u}
**C**_ROM = **C**_FEM **Φ** ∈ R^{n_y×200}

**Step 4.** The ROM is valid while the thermal state remains in the span of **Φ**. An alarm is raised when the state projection residual ||**x**_full − **Φ****Φ**^T **x**_full|| / ||**x**_full|| > 0.01 (1% residual threshold), signalling that an off-nominal scenario outside the ROM training space has occurred and the ground FEM must be consulted.

