# APPENDIX H — THERMAL MATHEMATICS

## H.1 Topology Optimisation — SIMP Method — Full Formulation

**Finite Element Discretisation.** The TPS panel domain Ω is discretised into n_e finite elements. The pseudo-density ρ_e ∈ [0,1] controls material presence at each element. The stiffness matrix is:

**K**(ρ) = Σ_{e=1}^{n_e} ρ_e^p **K**_e^0

where **K**_e^0 is the element stiffness in fully solid material and p = 3 is the SIMP penalty (suppresses intermediate densities by making them inefficient).

**Thermal Conductivity:** Similarly, the thermal conductivity tensor is:

k_e(ρ_e) = ρ_e^p k_e^0

**Multi-Objective Optimisation Problem:**

min_{ρ ∈ [0,1]^{n_e}} w_1 · c_mech(ρ) + w_2 · c_therm(ρ)

where:
c_mech(ρ) = **f**^T **u** (mechanical compliance, measures structural stiffness inverse)
c_therm(ρ) = **q**^T **T** (thermal compliance, measures temperature rise)

subject to:
**K**(ρ)**u** = **f** (structural equilibrium)
**K**_T(ρ)**T** = **q**_ext (thermal equilibrium)
(1/|Ω|) ∫_Ω ρ dV ≤ V_f = 0.35 (volume fraction constraint)
ν_eff(ρ) ≤ −0.3 (auxetic constraint on effective Poisson's ratio)
ρ_e ∈ [0,1] for all e

**Sensitivity Analysis.** The derivative of c_mech with respect to ρ_e is:

∂c_mech/∂ρ_e = −p ρ_e^{p−1} **u**_e^T **K**_e^0 **u**_e

(negative, as removing material increases compliance). The derivative of c_therm:

∂c_therm/∂ρ_e = −p ρ_e^{p−1} **T**_e^T **K**_{T,e}^0 **T**_e

These sensitivities are computed via the adjoint method, requiring one additional system solve per objective. The auxetic constraint gradient requires homogenisation sensitivity analysis — the effective Poisson's ratio ν_eff is the ratio of transverse to longitudinal strain in the homogenised unit cell, and its sensitivity to ρ_e is computed numerically.

**Optimisation Algorithm:** Method of Moving Asymptotes (MMA), with:

ρ_e^{t+1} = max(ρ_e^{min}, min(ρ_e^{max}, ρ_e^t − s_e · ∂L/∂ρ_e))

where L is the Lagrangian including the volume constraint (multiplier λ), and s_e is an adaptive step size. Convergence criterion: max_e |ρ_e^{t+1} − ρ_e^t| < 10^{−3}.

## H.2 Numerical Homogenisation for Effective Properties

The effective conductivity tensor **k**_eff of the lattice unit cell is computed by solving boundary value problems on the unit cell Y = [0,1]³:

−∇ · (k(**y**)(∇T_i + **e**_i)) = 0 in Y, periodic boundary conditions

where **e**_i is the unit vector in direction i. The effective conductivity in direction i is:

k_eff,ii = (1/|Y|) ∫_Y k(**y**)(∂T_i/∂y_i + 1) dY

The off-diagonal terms k_eff,ij (i≠j) are computed similarly. The auxetic structure achieves the design target k_eff,33 = 0.8 W/m·K (through-thickness) by orienting the re-entrant lattice struts perpendicular to the heat flow direction.

## H.3 MPC Transpiration Cooling — Full Formulation

**State Space ROM from FEM:**

**T**_{k+1} = **A**_d **T**_k + **B**_{d,q} q_{ext,k} + **B**_{d,c} ṁ''_{c,k} + **w**_k
**T**_max,k = **C**_max **T**_k

where **A**_d = e^{**A**·Δt} is the matrix exponential (discretised from continuous-time FEM), Δt = 0.1 s, **C**_max selects the maximum temperature node.

**MPC Problem over horizon N_p = 300 steps (30 s):**

min_{ṁ''_c(0),...,ṁ''_c(N_p−1)} J = Σ_{k=0}^{N_p−1} ṁ''_c(k)² · Δt + ρ_T · max(0, **C**_max **T**_k − T_limit)²

subject to:
**T**_{k+1} = **A**_d **T**_k + **B**_{d,q} q_k + **B**_{d,c} ṁ''_c(k)
**C**_max **T**_k ≤ T_limit = 1800 K for all k = 0,...,N_p
0 ≤ ṁ''_c(k) ≤ ṁ''_{c,max}

This is a convex QP in the decision variables [ṁ''_c(0),...,ṁ''_c(N_p−1)]:

**Substituting** the state equations forward: **T**_k = **A**_d^k **T**_0 + Σ_{j=0}^{k−1} **A**_d^{k−1−j}(**B**_{d,q} q_j + **B**_{d,c} ṁ''_c(j))

**Define:** decision vector **u** = [ṁ''_c(0),...,ṁ''_c(N_p−1)]^T ∈ R^{N_p}

The temperature at step k is linear in **u**: **C**_max **T**_k = **c**_k(T_0, q_{0:k}) + **h**_k^T **u**

where **h**_k = **C**_max [**A**_d^{k−1} **B**_{d,c}, **A**_d^{k−2} **B**_{d,c}, ..., **B**_{d,c}, **0**, ..., **0**]^T.

**The full QP:**

min_**u** **u**^T **u** · Δt
subject to **H****u** ≤ **b** (temperature constraints)
**0** ≤ **u** ≤ ṁ''_{c,max} **1** (physical bounds)

where **H** = [**h**_0^T; **h**_1^T; ...; **h**_{N_p−1}^T] and **b** = [T_limit − c_0; ...; T_limit − c_{N_p−1}].

This QP has n = N_p = 300 variables and 2N_p = 600 inequality constraints. Solved at 10 Hz using a warm-started active-set solver, computation time < 5 ms on the avionics processor.

